crates/fleet-lazygit/src/root/events.rs:6-33:
```rust
cx.spawn(async move |root, cx| {
    while let Ok(event) = events.recv().await {
        let mut batch = vec![event];
        for _ in 1..64 {
            let Ok(event) = events.try_recv() else { break };
            batch.push(event);
        }
        if root.update(cx, |root, cx| { … }).is_err() { return; }
    }        // <- loops straight back into recv(), no yield
})
```
`async_channel::Receiver::recv()` completes on its first poll whenever a message is buffered, so with a non-empty channel this whole `while` body runs inside a single task poll and the future never returns `Pending`. fleet-app's counterpart states the rule and fixes it — crates/fleet-app/src/shell/root/events.rs:155-158:
```rust
// A busy producer cannot keep the UI executor inside an always-ready receive loop.
cx.background_executor()
    .timer(Duration::from_millis(1))
    .await;
```
fleet-app also names its limit (`const EVENT_BATCH_LIMIT: usize = 128`, events.rs:16) where lazygit inlines a bare `64`. The producer here is unbounded (crates/fleet-lazygit/src/bridge.rs:385-386), so an arbitrarily deep backlog can be waiting when the loop starts.
